## 41. Granite 3.0 1B-A400M MoE residency (gates fixed before implementation)

The reference is IBM's official `granite-3.0-1b-a400m-instruct` checkpoint
at revision `ffec3c35bdfd97a06f0b4cd5fcc92cd9b1584445`. Its BF16
safetensors use the original `block_sparse_moe.input_linear`,
`output_linear` and `router.layer` names. Match the checkpoint's
[Transformers v4.46.1 implementation](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/v4.46.1/src/transformers/models/granitemoe/modeling_granitemoe.py),
including the 12× embedding, 0.22× residual, 1/64 attention, 1/6 logit
scales, grouped RoPE and top-8 softmax router. The resident reference keeps
BF16 weights and computes in FP32 on the CPU; no quantization or CUDA MoE
path is implied.

| Gate | Fixed requirement |
|---|---|
| Resident layout and admission | Validate exact config, tensor names, shapes and dtypes before loading; reject unsupported variants, quantization and CUDA explicitly. Budget weights, full FP32 KV, full-prefill workspace, load transient and safety margin before allocation. Preserve Qwen3/Qwen3.5 defaults. |
| Router and expert parity | On deterministic fixture and official weights, compare at least two positions' per-layer FP32 router logits, exact top-8 IDs and weighted expert outputs with the pinned HF implementation. Normalized maximum error ≤1e-4 and relative RMS ≤1e-5 for tensors; selected IDs must match exactly. |
| Whole-model parity | Compare embedding, all 24 layer outputs and full logits on short and ≥128-token prompts with normalized maximum error ≤1e-4 and relative RMS ≤1e-5. Require 100% greedy-token agreement for two real prompts of at least eight generated tokens each. Do not relax gates after failures. |
| State and real use | Tokenwise versus whole-prefill logits agree within the tensor gates. Repeated decode, clear/reset, prefix replay, independent sessions and late failure rollback remain correct. Demonstrate real `marin-run` text generation on the official checkpoint. |
| Resident cost | Report cold/warm load, TTFT, prefill/decode tok/s, peak RSS, model bytes and estimated KV under 4/8 GiB zero-swap scopes where feasible. A no-fit outcome must be an ordinary error, never an OOM kill. Record exact command, hardware and build. |
| Routing measurement | On fixed prose/code short and longer prompts, count unique selected experts per layer, top-k frequencies, hit/miss curves for per-layer cache sizes, estimated transferred bytes/token and measured resident prefill/decode time. Do not infer cache speed from a hit rate alone. |
| Cache entry decision | Prototype a bounded exact-weight per-layer RAM cache only if a feasible budget saves ≥25% peak resident RSS **and** measured routing projects miss-transfer time below 25% of resident decode latency on the measured local storage bandwidth for both prose and code. Compare with the same all-resident model and prompts; otherwise stop at the resident reference and report cache NO-GO. |
| Regression and project checks | Existing Qwen3/Qwen3.5 CPU/CUDA, planner, packed INT8, KV, snapshots and scalar tests pass. Run workspace fmt/check/test, all-feature Clippy `-D warnings`, line-limit, unsafe and final diff checks. No commits, pushes or stash changes. |

The cache decision uses a bandwidth measurement from this machine, not a
published Colibrì throughput number. Successful resident parity is a
prerequisite for routing measurements and any cache experiment.

### Resident implementation and HF evidence

The implementation loads official BF16 safetensors into Marin's existing
matrix storage, validates all 218 tensor names/shapes/dtypes, and reuses FP32
RMSNorm, RoPE, attention, SIMD/scalar GEMV and sampling. Only Granite's
checkpoint scales, top-8 router and grouped expert execution are new. All
24 layers run with growable FP32 KV; experts stay resident. CUDA and INT8
Granite requests fail before loading. At this stage prefill was tokenwise, so
one CPU core was effective even if a larger thread count was configured. The
official checkpoint is revision
`ffec3c35bdfd97a06f0b4cd5fcc92cd9b1584445` and the oracle is pinned
to Transformers 4.46.1.

```bash
/tmp/marin-granite-hf/bin/python tests/reference/granite/full.py \
  ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct bench-results/granite/hf-long.json --steps 8 --long
target/release/marin-run verify ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  --backend cpu --threads 1 --reference bench-results/granite/hf-long.json
```

The official chat-template greeting, code and 287-token prose prompts passed
all 24 routers (FP32 logits and exact top-8 IDs), weighted expert outputs,
embedding/all 24 layer outputs, full logits and **24/24 greedy IDs**. Worst
normalized maximum/relative RMS across these cases were
**4.825e-6/3.901e-6**, below the fixed **1e-4/1e-5** gates; worst absolute
logit difference was **3.43e-5**. The router/expert check observes each
prompt's last position, so it covers three positions across prompts.
Forced scalar GEMV initially missed one RMS gate (**1.262e-5** on a code
decode step). FP64 accumulation in the existing scalar dot fallback fixed
the drift; the same two-prompt, eight-step oracle then passed **16/16**
greedy IDs, with worst normalized maximum/relative RMS
**9.714e-6/8.783e-6**. SIMD kernels are unchanged; the shared scalar
fallback may pay a throughput cost from FP64 accumulation, not measured here.

The explicitly run real-checkpoint test was:

```bash
MARIN_GRANITE_MODEL=~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  cargo test -p model --test granite_real --release --locked -- --ignored --nocapture
```

It passed whole versus split prefill logits, decode continuation,
reset/replay, independent session and rollback after a deliberately malformed
cache failed following an earlier layer write. A real `marin-run` code prompt
with `--temp 0 --max-tokens 80` generated the complete function
`def add_two_numbers(num1, num2): return num1 + num2` in a Python fence.
That run measured **0.543 s TTFT**, **27.39 tok/s** decode, **2.73 GB**
peak RSS and **2.74 s** load. The requested limit truncated its subsequent
usage example, so this is a generation smoke test, not a code-quality claim.

### Resident cost and routing

Ryzen 7 260, 16 logical/8 physical cores, single CPU thread, release build,
AC power, official BF16 checkpoint (2,669,283,096-byte safetensors), one run
per row and no warmup. These are Marin measurements, not Colibrì results.
Synthetic `bench` prompts measure comparable lengths; the real
chat-template prompts above establish quality and routing. JSON is in
ignored `bench-results/granite/`.

```bash
target/release/marin-run bench ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  --backend cpu --threads 1 --prompt-tokens 128 --tokens 16 --ctx 256 \
  --runs 1 --warmup 0 --json bench-results/granite/bench-128.json
python3 -c 'import os; p=os.path.expanduser("~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct/model.safetensors"); f=os.open(p,os.O_RDONLY); os.posix_fadvise(f,0,0,os.POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED); os.close(f)'
time dd if=~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct/model.safetensors \
  of=/dev/null bs=4M iflag=direct status=none
```

| Prompt/output/context | Load | TTFT | Prefill | Decode | Peak RSS | KV held |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 16/16/128, page-cache warm | 2.07 s | 0.508 s | 31.5 tok/s | 28.05 tok/s | 2.73 GB | 13 MB |
| 128/16/256, page-cache warm | 2.09 s | 4.087 s | 31.3 tok/s | 27.60 tok/s | 2.73 GB | 25 MB |
| 512/8/768, page-cache warm | 2.08 s | 16.807 s | 30.5 tok/s | 25.20 tok/s | 2.77 GB | 75 MB |
| 16/8/128, file-cache evicted with `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` | 2.57 s | 0.543 s | 29.5 tok/s | 28.03 tok/s | 2.73 GB | 13 MB |

The evicted-file run recorded **2.54 GB read from disk**; warm runs recorded
0 B. `marin-run plan --backend cpu --ctx 512` estimated **3.11 GB**:
2.67 GB weights, 50 MB full-window FP32 KV, 17 MB full-prefill workspace,
101 MB largest-tensor load transient and 268 MB safety margin (rounded).
The measured 512-token process peak was **2.77 GB**. This host exposed no
enforceable cgroup-v2 memory scope and had swap enabled, so 4/8 GiB
zero-swap OOM behavior is **unverified**; the estimate is not an OOM test.

`granite_real` traced all 24 layers during four real prompt+eight-decode
runs. Every token selects eight of 32 experts per layer. A simulated
exact-weight per-layer LRU used the observed top-8 IDs; misses below are
counts, not measured file transfers.

| Workload | Prompt tokens | Unique experts/layer | LRU-8 hits | LRU-16 hits | LRU-22 hits | LRU-22 decode hits |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Prose short | 15 | 23–32, mean 29.04 | 1597/4416 (36.2%) | 2995/4416 (67.8%) | 3417/4416 (77.4%) | 1350/1536 (87.9%) |
| Code short | 17 | 22–31, mean 28.25 | 1681/4800 (35.0%) | 3103/4800 (64.6%) | 3758/4800 (78.3%) | 1310/1536 (85.3%) |
| Prose long | 287 | 28–32, mean 30.83 | 20805/56640 (36.7%) | 38491/56640 (68.0%) | 47358/56640 (83.6%) | 1277/1536 (83.1%) |
| Code long | 217 | 22–32, mean 29.67 | 10967/43200 (25.4%) | 30241/43200 (70.0%) | 37923/43200 (87.8%) | 1305/1536 (85.0%) |

The same four rendered prompts run through the final `marin-run -p --temp 0
--max-tokens 9 --ctx 512 --threads 1 --metrics` path yielded:

| Workload | TTFT | Prefill | Decode | Decode p50 | Peak RSS |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Prose short | 0.483 s | 31.0 tok/s | 25.56 tok/s | 37.6 ms | 2.73 GB |
| Code short | 0.539 s | 31.6 tok/s | 27.33 tok/s | 35.5 ms | 2.73 GB |
| Prose long | 9.37 s | 30.6 tok/s | 27.11 tok/s | 36.8 ms | 2.73 GB |
| Code long | 7.00 s | 31.0 tok/s | 27.26 tok/s | 36.8 ms | 2.73 GB |

Aggregated selection frequencies across the 32 expert IDs ranged
**1267–2569** on long prose and **940–1715** on long code. A BF16 expert is
3 MiB; all 24×32 experts occupy 2.25 GiB. Retaining 22 per layer would
remove **720 MiB** from the 2.73 GB process peak at best (**27.7%**), before
cache metadata, load buffers or OS page cache. It is the largest cache size
that can clear the 25% RSS-saving gate. Its long-prompt decode misses imply
**97.1 MiB/token** (prose) and **86.6 MiB/token** (code) transferred from
storage. A read-only 4 MiB-block `dd iflag=direct` scan of the same file took
0.567 s for 2.669 GB (**4.71 GB/s**); even this optimistic sequential
bandwidth gives **21.6/19.3 ms** transfer lower bounds. The matching long
prose/code resident decode p50 was **36.8 ms** each, so the fixed 25%
allowance is only **9.2 ms**. Small random expert reads would likely
be slower. No cache was implemented because the required latency gate fails
for both prose and code; reduced process RSS alone is insufficient.

### Stage 41 acceptance

| Fixed gate | Decision |
|---|---|
| Resident layout and admission | **PASS for official checkpoint.** Exact metadata validated, all-resident BF16 and CPU-only semantics enforced, 3.11 GB 512-token plan includes named costs. |
| Router and expert parity | **PASS.** Three real positions × 24 layers, exact top-8, normalized/RMS errors below fixed limits. |
| Whole-model parity | **PASS.** Short prose/code and 287-token prompt, all layers/full logits, 24/24 greedy IDs. |
| State and real use | **PASS.** Split/replay/decode/reset/isolation/partial-layer rollback real-checkpoint test; actual `marin-run` generation. |
| Resident cost | **PARTIAL.** Warm/file-cache-cold load, TTFT, rates, RSS and KV measured. Enforced 4/8 GiB zero-swap runs unavailable on this host. |
| Routing measurement | **PASS.** Four prose/code short/long traces, exact per-layer LRU simulation and local direct-read bandwidth. |
| Cache entry | **NO-GO.** LRU-22 projects ≥25% RSS saving but optimistic transfer lower bounds are 2.0–2.4× the allowed decode overhead. Prototype intentionally skipped. |
| Regression and project checks | **PASS.** Full `MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` suite passed on the RTX 5050, including Qwen3/Qwen3.5, packed INT8, KV, snapshots and tensor tests. Workspace fmt/check, all-feature Clippy `-D warnings`, line-limit, unsafe and diff checks passed. |

Final release checks also passed Qwen3-0.6B with forced scalar GEMV
(**48/48** greedy IDs) and Qwen3.5-2B-Base BF16 CPU (**16/16** IDs,
worst normalized maximum/RMS **1.283e-6/2.278e-6**). The final
Granite release binary repeated the three-prompt, 24/24-ID HF pass after
the plan/verification cleanup. No Granite CUDA path or quantization was
added; existing CUDA tests ran on the RTX 5050.

**GO for correct all-resident Granite CPU inference on this machine. NO-GO
for bounded per-layer RAM caching under the fixed gate.** The next useful
work is a focused batched MoE prefill speed study; 31 tok/s prefill is close
to one-token decode and the current path rereads selected expert matrices
for every prompt token. It must keep router and state parity. This stage does
not justify SSD streaming, prefetching, mixed precision or a generic MoE
memory manager.

## 42. Granite resident MoE prefill optimization: gates set before code

This section's gates were recorded before changing the Granite execution path.
Use the same official BF16 checkpoint, pinned HF oracle and single-thread
512/8/768 benchmark as §41; collect serial, page-cache-warm repeated runs and
report medians plus run-to-run spread. Compare short and 128-token prompts,
and check an 8-thread case. No simultaneous CPU benchmarks. Record power or
frequency when the host exposes them; otherwise state that limitation.

| Gate | Required result |
|---|---|
| HF numerical and routing parity | Exact top-8 IDs at every observed layer, normalized max ≤1e-4 and relative RMS ≤1e-5 for router, weighted experts, layers and logits; exact 24/24 reference greedy IDs. Thresholds unchanged from §41. |
| State and fallback | Real-checkpoint split prefill/decode, reset, rollback, session isolation and scalar path pass; Qwen3/Qwen3.5 CPU/CUDA regressions pass. |
| Main prefill target | 512-token prefill median ≥39.65 tok/s (≥30% above 30.5), TTFT ≤12.93 s (≥23% below 16.807 s), with no >3% median regression at short/128 tokens. |
| Decode, load and memory | Matched decode median ≥97% of the §41 25.20 tok/s baseline, warm load ≤2.18 s (+5%), peak RSS ≤3.03 GB (+~10% from 2.77 GB); admission estimate must cover any new workspace. |
| Smaller candidate | May retain a smaller gain only with repeated, controlled evidence of useful net latency benefit and negligible complexity; explicitly mark the main target missed. |

Profiling must separate attention, projections, routing, expert matmuls,
normalization, movement/allocation and scalar precision effects where
measurable. Hardware counters are optional; arithmetic traffic estimates are
not measurements. Reject candidates that fail correctness or material
regression gates rather than weakening these limits.

### Profile and implementation

The Ryzen 7 260 was on AC power; all runs were serial and used the official
BF16 checkpoint. `perf`, `valgrind`, `samply` and `turbostat` were unavailable.
Temporary per-stage `Instant` probes were built into the release binary for
one 128-token/2-output run of each path, then removed. The probes include
allocation and copy costs in their enclosing stages, so they cannot separate
those costs individually. CPU frequency and package power were not sampled.

| 128-token prefill stage, 1 thread | Original tokenwise | Grouped batch |
|---|---:|---:|
| Input norm + QKV + RoPE | 0.558 s | 0.131 s |
| KV writes/read + attention | 0.046 s (attention), 0.011 s (RoPE/KV) | 0.017 s |
| Attention output projection + residual | 0.270 s | 0.065 s |
| Post norm + router + top-8 | 0.023 s | 0.005 s |
| Expert input/output matmuls, activation, gather/scatter | 3.206 s | 0.907 s |
| Full TTFT in the probed run | 4.114 s | 1.136 s |

The original expert stage was about 78% of measured TTFT; the grouped path's
expert stage remains about 80%. A one-token original decode probe attributed
0.024 s to experts, 0.004 s to QKV and 0.002 s to output projection. The
normal 128-token decode benchmark estimated 73.6 GB/s of weight traffic;
that is the harness's bytes/time estimate, not a hardware bandwidth counter.
The forced scalar path uses FP64 dot accumulation for parity; the normal AVX2
path does not. No FP64 stage cost was measurable in the SIMD run.

For prompts of at least 32 tokens, Granite now runs layers across all prompt
rows, computes batched attention/projections, sorts the unchanged exact top-8
router result into per-expert token groups, invokes existing BF16 SIMD/threaded
`Matrix::matmul`, and scatters weighted outputs in ascending expert-ID order.
The short/decode path remains tokenwise. Existing FP32 KV is written per layer
and committed only after all layers succeed; failure truncates to the prior
length. The new activation/routing buffer estimate is admitted before
allocation, using the larger of the Granite and existing CPU workspace
estimates plus the existing KV growth/attention packing/safety costs. This
does not change checkpoint storage or load work.

### Controlled before/after

`marin-run bench` used `--backend cpu --threads 1 --warmup 0 --runs 2`, greedy
sampling, 16 output tokens for 16/128 prompts and 8 for 512; contexts were
128/256/768 respectively. The 512 original/optimized pair was remeasured in
this stage; 16/128 original numbers are the one-run §41 baselines, with a
separate original 128-token profile also recording 31.1 tok/s and 4.114 s.
Page cache was warm (`disk_read_bytes=0`); each row's load was a new process.
The JSON is in ignored `bench-results/granite/opt-*.json`.

```bash
target/release/marin-run bench ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  --backend cpu --threads 1 --prompt-tokens 512 --tokens 8 --ctx 768 \
  --runs 2 --warmup 0 --json bench-results/granite/opt-final-512.json
```

| Prompt, path | TTFT | Prefill | Decode | Warm load | Peak RSS |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 16, original (§41) | 0.508 s | 31.5 tok/s | 28.05 tok/s | 2.07 s | 2.73 GB |
| 16, final 1 thread | 0.509 s | 31.4 tok/s | 27.48 tok/s | 2.08 s | 2.73 GB |
| 128, original (§41) | 4.087 s | 31.3 tok/s | 27.60 tok/s | 2.09 s | 2.73 GB |
| 128, final 1 thread | 1.067 s | 120.0 tok/s | 27.40 tok/s | 2.03 s | 2.73 GB |
| 512, original 1 thread, two runs | 16.687 s | 30.7 tok/s | 25.62 tok/s | 2.02 s | 2.77 GB |
| 512, final 1 thread, two runs | 3.900 s | 131.3 tok/s | 25.90 tok/s | 2.09 s | 2.78 GB |
| 512, final 8 threads, two runs | 1.319 s | 388.2 tok/s | 25.57 tok/s | 2.06 s | 2.79 GB |

The paired 512-token result is **4.28×** the original prefill rate and
**76.6%** lower TTFT with one thread. The two original TTFT runs were
16.679–16.694 s; final 1-thread runs were 3.890–3.909 s; final
8-thread runs were 1.307–1.331 s. These narrow ranges, identical AC setting
and serial runs support the large prefill win, though frequency/thermal
telemetry was unavailable. The 8-thread prefill uses about 3.6 of 16 logical
cores on average; its 25.57 tok/s decode is effectively unchanged from the
§41 25.20 tok/s baseline. Peak RSS increased by 0.01–0.02 GB at 512 tokens,
well below the 0.26 GB allowance. No expert cache or decode arithmetic
change was attempted in this milestone.

The temporary probes were removed; uninstrumented final-source runs supply
the acceptance rates above. No `perf` cache-miss or separate allocation
breakdown is claimed.

### Acceptance and next bottleneck

| Fixed gate | Decision and evidence |
|---|---|
| Router, expert, layers, logits, generation | **PASS.** Real HF long oracle: all observed layer routers/expert outputs and full logits passed, exact top-8, 24/24 greedy IDs; worst normalized max/RMS 4.825e-6/3.901e-6. |
| Scalar fallback | **PASS.** Forced scalar real HF oracle gave exact 24/24 greedy IDs; worst normalized max/RMS 8.216e-6/6.902e-6. |
| State and transaction | **PASS.** Real-checkpoint test covers whole/split prefill, append/decode, reset/replay, independent sessions and malformed-cache rollback; a 32-token batch is compared with 32 individual token passes. |
| Main 512-token prefill | **PASS.** 131.3 tok/s vs paired 30.7, 3.900 s TTFT vs 16.687; eight threads reach 388.2 tok/s. |
| Short/128 regression | **PASS with measurement limit.** Short TTFT +0.2%, prefill −0.3%, decode −2.0% versus one-run §41 data; 128 improves 3.83×. The short path's arithmetic is unchanged; its baseline was not repeated in this stage. |
| Decode, load, RSS | **PASS.** 512 one-thread decode 25.90 vs 25.20 tok/s gate baseline; eight-thread decode 25.57, load 2.03–2.09 s, peak 2.78–2.79 GB. |
| Project checks and regressions | **PASS.** `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, workspace/all-target `cargo check`, all-feature Clippy `-D warnings`, line-limit, unsafe and diff checks; `MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` passed on RTX 5050. The ignored official Granite test was run separately in release mode. |
| Rejected/deferred candidate | **NO-GO for decode micro-optimization here.** The 1-token profile concentrates 24 ms in expert matmuls; router/norm and small per-token allocations are not a credible next win. No decode candidate was coded, and no cache, new precision or extra kernel was introduced. |

**GO for grouped, batched Granite CPU prefill.** It clears the fixed speed,
quality and memory gates with one small architecture-specific path. The next
measured bottleneck is expert GEMM (0.907 s of 1.136 s at 128 tokens); any
future work should profile its BF16 weight traffic and tile efficiency before
altering decode or cache policy. Strictly controlled 4/8 GiB OOM tests and
hardware cache-miss/power counters remain unavailable on this host, as in §41.

## 43. Granite Q8, CUDA MoE and larger-model feasibility: gates fixed before implementation

These gates were recorded before changing export, loading or execution code. Use
the official Granite 3.0 1B-A400M-Instruct checkpoint, group-128 Q8, the
existing §41 HF prompts and §42 benchmark settings. Run CPU and GPU benchmarks
serially after a warm-up. Report raw values, spread and any unverified gate;
do not treat a missing comparison as a pass.

| Phase | Acceptance gate |
|---|---|
| A: packed Q8 integrity | One-time export atomically publishes a versioned artifact; direct load checks architecture, group, tensor shapes, checksums and truncation. Corruption/incomplete exports fail without BF16 fallback. Resident matrices remain Q8; router and norms retain source precision and the existing exact top-k algorithm. |
| A: matched numerical parity | An independently quantized HF reference uses the same source tensors, group-128 symmetric max-absolute rounding, exact router weights and FP32 normalization. At observed router, expert, layer and logit outputs: normalized max ≤1e-4, relative RMS ≤1e-5; route IDs and 24/24 greedy tokens match that quantized reference. |
| A: BF16 quality | On fixed prose/code samples, ≥95% top-1 agreement and ≥95% greedy-token agreement with BF16; no newly failing executable code case. Report any routing differences from BF16 rather than forcing identical routes. |
| A: useful efficiency | Artifact ≤1.50 GB; 512-token peak RSS ≤1.85 GB (BF16 2.78 GB); 128/512 decode median ≥1.2× matched BF16 and 512 prefill ≥90% of 131.3 tok/s (1 thread) and 388.2 tok/s (8 threads). Warm load ≤2.2 s; report one-time export and cold-load separately. |
| B: CUDA correctness | Real BF16 model: same §41 normalized max/RMS ≤1e-4/1e-5 and exact 24/24 HF greedy IDs, exact top-8 route IDs; split prefill/decode, rollback, reset and session isolation pass. Expert weights/state stay device-resident and per-token routing requires no host round trip. GPU Q8 must meet the same quantized-HF and quality gates if supported. |
| B: CUDA fit and speed | All-resident weights plus KV and workspace fit actual free RTX 5050 VRAM with the existing admission margin; no guard bypass. On 128/512 prompts, report TTFT, prefill/decode, VRAM, process RSS and GPU power/clocks where available; median 512 prefill and decode each ≥matched CPU BF16 one-thread results without a >10% short-prompt regression. |
| C: feasibility | Use official config/tensor inventories for a sparse model with substantially larger total than active weights. Separate dense, expert and state bytes, active bytes/token, RAM/VRAM admission and measured-or-clearly-estimated transfer time. Recommend a residency pilot only if the fit and transfer math justify it. No streaming implementation in this milestone. |

Failure of a phase's correctness gate blocks subsequent implementation phases;
an efficiency miss is reported as NO-GO for that optimization rather than
silently relaxing the threshold.

### Phase A: real Granite packed Q8 pilot (2026-10-14)

The official BF16 Instruct model was exported with `marin-run export-int8`
using the existing Q8 row-group format. Dense and expert matrices use INT8
codes plus FP32 scales. The router is copied as BF16-exact FP32, and norms
remain FP32. Direct loading validates the packed tensor set/shapes/group and
per-tensor SHA-256 while reading; it never materializes the full BF16 model.
The CPU SIMD and scalar Q8 kernels, batched MoE prefill, FP32 KV and existing
top-8 routing are reused. An ignored official-checkpoint test confirmed
source-to-Q8 and direct-packed Q8 produce *bit-identical* prompt, decode and
reset logits. A flipped byte was rejected at load with a checksum mismatch;
wrong group and missing explicit `--quantize int8` were rejected. The existing
packed writer's temporary-directory, sync and atomic-rename rules are reused.

```bash
marin-run export-int8 ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct-Q8-pilot --quant-group 128
marin-run ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct-Q8-pilot \
  --backend cpu --quantize int8 --ctx 128 --temp 0 --max-tokens 8 \
  -p $'<|start_of_role|>user<|end_of_role|>Reply with a brief greeting.<|end_of_text|>\n<|start_of_role|>assistant<|end_of_role|>'
marin-run verify ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct-Q8-pilot \
  --backend cpu --quantize int8 --reference /tmp/granite-q8-hf-long.json
```

The independent reference uses Transformers 4.46.1/CPU FP32, loading the
official BF16 checkpoint and replacing every eligible matrix with
`round_away_from_zero(weight / (maxabs/127)) * scale` per group of 128. Router
weights and norms are untouched. A separate safetensors comparison found **0
code and 0 scale differences across all 145 quantized tensors** between this
reference conversion and Marin's artifact. The extended HF trace includes
decode-layer and expert values; BF16 passes that extended trace for all three
prompts and 24 generated steps.

| Phase A gate | Evidence and decision |
|---|---|
| Integrity/storage | **PASS.** 1,382,441,536-byte complete artifact (51.7% of the 2,672,765,570-byte source), below 1.50 GB. Corruption/group/opt-in rejection and exact source-vs-packed Q8 execution passed. |
| Matched quantized-HF parity | **FAIL.** Greeting: 8/8 greedy steps, worst normalized max/RMS 4.370e-6/3.186e-6. Code: step 2 full-logit relative RMS **1.175e-5** (limit 1e-5), normalized max 3.573e-6. Long 287-token prompt: first incorrect hidden layer **9**, relative RMS **2.523e-5**. Top-8 IDs match through the inspected failing short decode. Forced scalar also fails (first incorrect expert layer 1 at step 2, RMS 1.327e-5), so this is not isolated to AVX2. Exact codes/scales and passing BF16 trace rule out an export-layout or reference-trace mismatch; accumulated FP32 arithmetic differences remain the leading, unproven cause. |
| Quality vs BF16 | **PARTIAL PASS.** Independent matched-HF BF16 and Q8 greedy continuations agree at 23/24 positions across greeting, code and 287-token prose prompts (95.83%, above 95%). Executable code correctness, perplexity and Marin's complete 24-step Q8 matched-HF sequence were not verified, so the full gate is open. |
| Memory/startup/speed | **PASS on measured cases.** Q8 artifact 1.382 GB; 512-token peak RSS 1.49 GB, down from 2.78–2.79 GB. Median warm load 1.26–1.28 s vs BF16 1.91–1.92 s. Q8 one-time export took 4.13 s to NVMe from a page-cache-warm source (4.79 s to tmpfs). One fadvise-cold read measured Q8 1.42 s/1.38 GB disk reads vs BF16 2.42 s/2.54 GB; it is a single run, not a distribution. |

All serial 512-token runs below used the same final binary on AC power,
`--ctx 768 --tokens 8 --runs 2 --warmup 0`, after the files were page-cache
warm. JSON is in ignored `bench-results/granite/q8-*.json`.

| Weights, threads | TTFT median (range) | Prefill tok/s | Decode tok/s | Load | Peak RSS |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| BF16, 1 | 3.979 s (3.978–3.980) | 128.7 | 25.38 | 1.91 s | 2.78 GB |
| Q8, 1 | 3.995 s (3.983–4.008) | 128.2 | 43.56 | 1.26 s | 1.49 GB |
| BF16, 8 | 1.340 s (1.334–1.347) | 382.0 | 24.82 | 1.92 s | 2.79 GB |
| Q8, 8 | 1.283 s (1.274–1.292) | 399.2 | 43.13 | 1.28 s | 1.49 GB |

At 128 tokens/one thread Q8 measured TTFT 1.101 s, prefill 116.3 tok/s,
decode 48.45 tok/s, warm load 1.30 s and peak RSS 1.43 GB (two runs). This
meets the fixed efficiency gates: 512 prefill is 99.6%/104.5% of matched BF16
for 1/8 threads and decode is 1.72×/1.74×, while memory falls about 46.4%.
The BF16-source, on-the-fly Q8 planner now estimates **1.38 GB resident
weights plus source-tensor conversion transient**, rather than claiming the
full BF16 source size remains resident. At `--ctx 768` it reports 1.85 GB
needed. One source-to-Q8 128-token run read 2.55 GB from disk, loaded in
3.85 s and peaked at 1.45 GB RSS; it is a single cold-ish read, not a
controlled startup distribution. The direct packed artifact is the measured
fast-start path.
The harness's 60–67 GB/s estimated Q8 decode traffic is bytes/time, not a
hardware bandwidth counter; power/clocks were not sampled. The prompt TTFT
does not improve at one thread because prefill is nearly unchanged.

**Phase A: NO-GO for acceptance.** Real opt-in CPU Q8 loads and runs, and
clears the measured storage, speed and RAM gates, but the fixed independent
HF numerical gate fails. BF16 remains the default; this packed Q8 path is an
unaccepted pilot. **Phase B: BLOCKED, CUDA Granite MoE was not implemented.**
The existing Qwen3/Qwen3.5 CUDA paths remain untouched. No GPU performance,
VRAM, power, state or rollback claim for Granite is made. The failed
numerical gate must be resolved before a GPU implementation can be based on
this phase; the nearest useful experiment is a narrow operator/layer
accumulation study at the first failing long-prompt layer and decode expert,
with the same independent HF threshold.

Final-source checks passed: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, workspace/all-target
`cargo check`, all-target/all-feature Clippy `-D warnings`,
`MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` on the
RTX 5050, `scripts/check-line-limit.sh`, `scripts/check-unsafe.sh` and
`git diff --check`. The ignored release-mode real-checkpoint Q8 source/direct
load equality test also passed. BF16 extended-HF parity passed 3/3 prompts;
Q8 extended-HF parity failed as above. No GPU Granite test was run because
there is no CUDA MoE implementation.

### Preliminary larger-model feasibility (research only; Phase C not accepted)

[IBM's Granite 3.0 3B-A800M](https://www.ibm.com/new/announcements/ibm-granite-3-0-open-state-of-the-art-enterprise-models)
has 40 experts and 800M active parameters. A roughly 3 GB Q8 artifact would
usually fit this 8 GB GPU and does not by itself establish a selective
residency need. [IBM's Granite 4.0 H-Tiny config](https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-4.0-h-tiny/blob/main/config.json)
specifies 40 hybrid layers, hidden width 1536, 64 experts/layer, top-6 and
expert width 512; [the official files](https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-4.0-h-tiny/tree/main)
total about 13.9 GB BF16. From those dimensions, expert matrices alone are
6,039,797,760 parameters, or 12.08 GB BF16 / estimated 6.23 GB group-128
Q8. The six active experts per layer imply ~584 MB Q8 expert bytes per
token, excluding dense/shared weights. The remaining BF16 files are about
1.8 GB; estimated all-resident Q8 total is about 7.2 GB before GPU state,
workspace and safety, exceeding the RTX 5050's **6,822 MiB free** as read from
`nvidia-smi` after the benchmark run (8,151 MiB total).
At **hypothetical** 8/16 GB/s host-to-device bandwidth, transferring all
active expert bytes each token costs at least 73/36 ms before computation;
actual PCIe throughput, layer reuse and miss cost were not measured. Its
Mamba/recurrent blocks and shared expert also require new model validation,
so it is a later research candidate, not a drop-in Granite 3.0 extension.
An 8 GiB host also cannot safely hold an estimated 7.2 GB Q8 artifact plus
runtime and safety headroom. No streaming pilot is justified from these
estimates. The concrete next milestone is to close the Granite Q8 numerical
gap first, then validate all-resident CUDA BF16 before measuring selective
residency on a genuinely memory-constrained sparse model.

## 44. Granite Q8 parity closure and independent CUDA BF16 gates (fixed before code)

This milestone keeps §43's numerical and efficiency thresholds unchanged.
The CPU Q8 diagnosis and CUDA BF16 implementation are independent: failure
of Q8 parity does not block BF16 CUDA. CUDA Q8 is outside this milestone.
Use the official Granite 3.0 1B-A400M-Instruct weights, the same independent
quantized HF oracle, and the same §41 prose/code/long prompts.

| Gate | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| CPU Q8 root cause | Reproduce the 287-token layer-9 mismatch; compare router, selected expert input, individual Q8 projection outputs, expert weighting/accumulation, and layer output with the independent quantized HF run. State the first divergent operation and its numerical mechanism. |
| CPU Q8 parity and quality | §43's normalized max ≤1e-4, relative RMS ≤1e-5 at router, expert, layer and full logits; exact top-8 and 24/24 greedy IDs against quantized HF. Keep ≥95% top-1/greedy agreement with BF16 and the executable-code gate. Do not change quantization codes, scales, or tolerances. |
| CUDA BF16 correctness | Independently meet §43's normalized max ≤1e-4, relative RMS ≤1e-5 at real-model layers/logits; exact top-8 and 24/24 HF greedy IDs. Split prefill/decode, reset, rollback, and independent sessions agree with whole-prompt execution. Expert weights and routing/state remain on GPU without per-token host transfer. |
| CUDA fit and performance | Existing VRAM admission margin must cover all resident weights, KV and workspace. On short, 128 and 512-token prompts report serial-run TTFT, prefill/decode tok/s, peak process RSS/VRAM, load, power/clocks if available, and compare to matched CPU BF16/Q8. §43's median 512 prefill and decode ≥CPU BF16 one-thread, short TTFT regression ≤10%, remain the acceptance thresholds. |
| Regression checks | BF16/INT8 opt-in/default behavior, Qwen3/Qwen3.5 CPU/CUDA, scalar fallback, threading, KV rollback and snapshots; required fmt, check, Clippy, full tests, line-limit and unsafe checks. |

### CPU Q8 diagnosis (2026-10-14)

The packed Q8 verifier reproduced §43 without changing the artifact or gate:
the 287-token prose prompt first fails its observed hidden output at layer 9
(relative RMS **2.523e-5**), and code decode step 2 fails full logits at
**1.175e-5**. Temporary instrumentation, removed after the experiment,
recorded the last token's post-attention MoE input, router, eight individual
expert projections and outputs, and per-layer mixtures. The reference was a
fresh Transformers 4.46.1 FP32 run with the independent group-128 quantizer;
the artifact's 145 code/scale tensor pairs had already been proven bit-identical
to that quantizer in §43.

At layer 9, the Q8 `Matrix::matmul` result for each of the eight selected
experts differed from independent Torch matrix-vector multiplication fed the
**same Marin input** by only **2.20e-7–2.68e-7 relative RMS**. The input to
that layer already differed from HF by **3.23e-5**; its router differed by
**1.02e-5**, expert input projections by **1.79e-5–2.75e-5**, and individual
expert outputs by **3.45e-5–6.60e-5** against HF's different input. The
selected IDs at the inspected layer were the same `{0,5,7,8,9,15,25,29}`.
Thus the layer-9 Q8 operator and packed layout are not the source of the
observed error; forcing a different dequantization or rewriting Q8 would be
misdirected.

The first observed differences occur in layer 0:
attention **4.08e-7** and MoE mixture **8.77e-7** relative RMS. They grow
through the recurrent sequence of attention and expert/residual operations:
layer-6 attention **1.13e-5**, mixture **1.22e-5**; layer-8 attention
**7.43e-5**, mixture **4.79e-5**. This is the first observed threshold
crossing in the operator trace, before the layer-9 hidden-state gate. The
pattern is consistent with different FP32 reduction order being amplified
by the nonlinear layers, but it does **not** prove one incorrect arithmetic
instruction. The existing scalar Q8 fallback
also fails the strict gate (§43), so an AVX2-only fix is ruled out. No Q8
kernel or tolerance was changed. CPU Q8 remains opt-in and **NO-GO for
acceptance**; executable-code quality and the full 24-step matched-HF sequence
remain unverified.

### Independent all-resident CUDA BF16 execution

Explicit `--backend cuda` now loads the official BF16 checkpoint directly
into 24 GPU layers. All 32 experts per layer remain resident as two stacked
BF16 matrices; the GPU computes exact top-8 choices and softmax weights,
selected expert projections and gated activations, and accumulates weighted
outputs in ascending expert-ID order. Router and expert intermediates stay on
device during normal generation. The only per-token host transfer is the
existing final-logit download for sampling. Attention uses the existing FP32
GPU KV cache, and the same cache truncation API handles whole-call rollback.
The planner admits resident weights, startup/window KV and a bounded 512-token
workspace with its existing VRAM safety margin. The explicit path rejects
INT8 and non-FP32 KV. `auto` continues to choose CPU for Granite while the
strict CUDA gate is open; BF16 remains the default precision.
The Granite GPU window budget rounds KV capacity up to whole eight-page
allocation slabs; a 768-token window needs two slabs rather than only its
75 MB logical payload.

```bash
target/release/marin-run verify ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  --backend cuda --reference bench-results/granite/hf-long.json
target/release/marin-run bench ~/models/Granite-3.0-1B-A400M-Instruct \
  --backend cuda --prompt-tokens 512 --tokens 8 --ctx 768 \
  --runs 2 --warmup 0 --json bench-results/granite/cuda-bf16-final-512.json
```

The strict verifier passes the greeting and 287-token prose prompts including
their eight decode steps. It rejects the code prompt at decode step 2:
full-logit normalized maximum **3.380e-6** is below 1e-4, but relative RMS
**1.098e-5** exceeds the fixed 1e-5 limit. The code step-2 layer-24 hidden
RMS was **7.95e-6**, while the final head output was **1.098e-5**: error was
already present before the head and was amplified by it. Dense projections
use the existing precise CUDA matmul; tensor-core projection rounding had
failed earlier at layer 8 (code) and layer 2 (long prompt). Separating GPU
residual multiplication from addition improved the code-logit RMS from
**1.173e-5** to **1.098e-5**, consistent with HF's separate operations, but
did not clear the gate. An FP64 expert-dot trial reached **1.074e-5** yet
reduced 128-token prefill from **465.3** to **84.2 tok/s** and was removed.
An FP64 LM-head trial did not improve the earlier **1.173e-5** failure and
was also removed. No numerical threshold was relaxed. The dedicated real
checkpoint state test passes whole/split prefill, decode, truncate/replay,
reset, independent sessions, GPU route-ID checking, invalid-token rejection
and rollback after a later chunk fails. The separate full 24-step greedy test
matched the same reference's **24/24 greedy IDs** across three prompts.
A direct CUDA-logit division trial gave code-step-2 RMS **1.100e-5**, slightly
worse than **1.098e-5**, so it was removed.

Serial release-mode GPU benchmarks used the same Ryzen 7 260 / RTX 5050
laptop on AC power, 40 W GPU limit, two runs after zero warm-up, warm file
cache. The 512-token CPU comparisons are the matched one-thread BF16/Q8 rows
in §43; 16/128 CPU rows are earlier §42/§43 local measurements on the same
machine and are not a paired final-binary run. Q8 is shown for efficiency
only because its numerical gate failed. The `bench`
process RSS is process peak; VRAM below is device-wide unless specified.

| Prompt, backend/weights | TTFT median (range) | Prefill tok/s | Decode tok/s | Load | Peak RSS | Peak VRAM |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 16, CUDA BF16 | 0.029 s (0.028–0.030) | 551.3 | 184.91 | 1.65 s | 0.307 GB | 3.74 GB device-wide |
| 16, CPU BF16, 1 thread (§42) | 0.509 s | 31.4 | 27.48 | 2.08 s | 2.73 GB | none |
| 128, CUDA BF16 | 0.276 s (0.262–0.290) | 465.3 | 184.64 | 1.62 s | 0.307 GB | 3.74 GB device-wide |
| 128, CPU BF16, 1 thread (§42) | 1.067 s | 120.0 | 27.40 | 2.03 s | 2.73 GB | none |
| 128, CPU Q8, 1 thread (§43) | 1.101 s | 116.3 | 48.45 | 1.30 s | 1.43 GB | none |
| 512, CUDA BF16 | 1.521 s (1.418–1.625) | 338.1 | 156.07 | 1.62 s | 0.307 GB | 3.85 GB device-wide |
| 512, CPU BF16, 1 thread (§43) | 3.979 s | 128.7 | 25.38 | 1.91 s | 2.78 GB | none |
| 512, CPU Q8, 1 thread (§43) | 3.995 s | 128.2 | 43.56 | 1.26 s | 1.49 GB | none |

CUDA BF16 clears §43's speed gate versus one-thread CPU BF16: 512-token
prefill is **2.63×**, decode **6.15×**, and TTFT **2.62×** better. The
16-token TTFT is far below the §42 CPU BF16 0.509 s baseline. Process RAM is
small because weights live on the GPU. One separate 512-token telemetry run
sampled up to **2,744 MiB per-process VRAM** via `nvidia-smi
--query-compute-apps`, versus 3,604 MiB device-wide; the two-run benchmark's
device-wide peak was 3.85 GB. Fourteen coarse samples spanning load and work
found power **13.21–41.47 W**, SM clocks **592–1,905 MHz**, and memory clocks
**9,001–12,001 MHz**. These are device readings, not process energy; the
40 W cap and clock range limit inference about run-to-run TTFT spread.

One additional `bench --profile` 128-token pass measured **252.83 ms / 89.2%**
of GPU prefill in router plus experts, **17.03 ms / 6.0%** in QKV projections,
**7.94 ms / 2.8%** in attention output projection, and **2.79 ms / 1.0%** in
attention. Decode spent **3.19 of 6.51 ms/token (49.0%)** in router/experts,
0.60 ms in QKV and 0.57 ms in attention. Prompt passes launched 461 kernels;
one-token passes 485. The custom selected-expert GEMV reads expert weights
repeatedly in prefill, so expert execution is the measured next bottleneck.
No kernel optimization is justified until strict parity clears.

| §44 gate | Decision |
|---|---|
| CPU Q8 first divergence | **DIAGNOSED, NOT FIXED.** Same-input Q8 expert matmul is within 2.68e-7 RMS of Torch; accumulated input/attention error crosses the gate before hidden layer 9. No single incorrect Q8 operation was found. |
| CPU Q8 numerical/quality | **FAIL / INCOMPLETE.** Layer-9 2.523e-5 and code-step-2 logits 1.175e-5 exceed 1e-5; §43 quality agreement is 23/24 but executable-code and full matched-HF sequence are open. |
| CUDA BF16 HF tensors | **FAIL.** Greeting and long prompt pass, but code decode step 2 full-logit RMS 1.098e-5 exceeds 1e-5. |
| CUDA BF16 greedy/state | **PASS.** 24/24 greedy IDs match HF; real checkpoint whole/split/reset/isolation/rollback test passes and verifier confirms route IDs where inspected. |
| CUDA fit and speed | **PASS for measured workloads.** Explicit admission and measured 512-token 338.1/156.07 tok/s prefill/decode clear the original one-thread CPU BF16 gates; no process-attributable power/energy counter. |
| Regression checks | **PASS.** `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked`, Clippy with `-D warnings`, `MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` (630 passed, 0 failed, 4 intentionally ignored real-model tests), line-limit, unsafe, and `git diff --check` pass. Both ignored official CUDA Granite greedy/state tests pass separately in release mode. |

**NO-GO for accepting CPU Q8 numerical parity and CUDA BF16 as default.**
The CUDA BF16 model is usable behind explicit `--backend cuda`, with genuine
all-resident GPU routing and large measured speed gains, but the fixed
numerical gate still fails narrowly. BF16 CPU remains the accepted Granite
default. The next work should isolate decode-step attention/expert rounding
with identical layer inputs before changing kernels; the 89% MoE prefill
bottleneck is an optimization target only after correctness passes. No cache,
streaming, INT4 or scheduler work was added.

Final-source CLI smoke test with the explicit CUDA command in README generated
`Hello! How can I assist you today?` for the greeting prompt. `marin-run plan`
selected CPU by default, admitted explicit BF16 CUDA at 128 tokens with
2.73 GB startup/full-window budget plus 512 MiB GPU margin, and rejected
explicit CUDA INT8. The rebuilt release verifier reproduced the fixed
code-step-2 failure at **1.098e-5**; the separate release greedy/state tests
both passed. Three pre-existing stashes were left untouched.

## 45. Granite numerical closure attempt (2026-10-15)

The §43–44 gates remain fixed: router, expert, layer and full-logit normalized
maximum ≤1e-4 and relative RMS ≤1e-5 against pinned Transformers 4.46.1
FP32 references, exact routing and 24/24 greedy IDs, plus the original Q8
quality, speed and memory thresholds. CUDA must retain the §43 512-token
prefill/decode floor versus one-thread CPU BF16 and avoid a >10% short-TTFT
regression. This pass investigates arithmetic only; an unverified numerical
change or a speed/memory regression is rejected. Run matched 128/512-token
BF16 CPU, Q8 CPU and BF16 CUDA benchmarks serially after the diagnosis.

### Pinned-reference arithmetic trace

Recreated the independent Q8 oracle from the official BF16 checkpoint with
Torch 2.5.1+cpu, Transformers 4.46.1, FP32 eager attention and the existing
group-128 quantizer in `tests/reference/granite/full.py`. The BF16 reference
used the same versions and all three §41 prose/code prompts. Temporary
last-token operator dumps compared Marin and HF on **identical inputs**;
all diagnostic code was removed afterward. The artifact's codes/scales and
top-k routing remain as verified in §43–44.

For the 287-token **Q8** prose prompt, the initial embedding is identical.
Layer 0's first measurable mismatch is RMSNorm (**1.69e-7 relative RMS**),
followed by Q/K/V projections (**2.45e-7 / 1.92e-7 / 2.94e-7**), the
post-attention residual (**6.54e-7**) and MoE mixture (**8.76e-7**).
The layer-0 output differs by **8.77e-7**. By layer 8 the incoming last-token
state differs by **7.81e-6**. Feeding Marin's **entire layer-8 input matrix**
to the pinned HF layer gives an output only **3.82e-7** from Marin, while
Marin versus the unmodified HF run's layer-8 output is **2.523e-5**. The
apparent layer-9 gate failure is therefore amplification of the incoming
state, not a faulty Q8 expert kernel at that layer. At code decode step 2,
the final hidden differs by **6.82e-6**. HF final norm plus head, fed that
same Marin hidden, differs from Marin's logits by only **3.21e-7**; its
distance from unmodified HF logits is **1.168e-5**, versus Marin's
**1.175e-5**. Replacing the head cannot repair the inherited difference.

For **CUDA BF16** code decode step 2, layer-0 input and RMSNorm are
bit-identical to HF. The first measurable differences are Q/K/V projections
(**2.69e-7 / 2.28e-7 / 3.76e-7 relative RMS**). The post-attention
residual differs by **2.35e-7**, MoE mixture by **6.38e-7**, and layer-0
output by **8.74e-7**. All observed layer tensors stay below the fixed gate;
the layer-24 hidden differs by **7.95e-6**. The HF final norm/head fed that
same hidden differs from Marin's logits by only **2.74e-7**, but its distance
from unmodified HF logits is **1.100e-5**, versus Marin's **1.098e-5**.
Thus the final head is not the cause of the logit miss. The existing CUDA
one-warp-per-row projection and selected-expert reductions do not use Torch
CPU's reduction tree. These small, input-dependent FP32 rounding differences
are amplified across 24 layers and by the head; no wrong expert ID, weight
layout, quantization code/scale or residual formula was observed.

Two controlled hypotheses failed. Moving Granite's attention multiplier to
the query side of HF gave **bit-identical** layer outputs: the multiplier is
a power of two and this is not the cause. Temporarily using the existing
CUDA general attention kernel for one-token decode improved the failing
logit RMS from **1.098e-5** to **1.062e-5**, still above **1e-5**; the
decode change was removed. §44's FP64 expert-dot trial was slower by over
5× and still failed. Matching the pinned Torch reduction order across norm,
projections, attention and expert accumulation would require a broader,
unproven arithmetic path and risks the fixed speed gates. No production
arithmetic was changed in this attempt. The precise blocker is the
accumulation of sub-micro-scale cross-backend rounding differences under a
whole-model **1e-5** RMS gate, not a demonstrated semantic bug to patch.
The trace identifies the first differing operations and the amplification;
it does not attribute the final miss to one specific instruction in every
layer.

The rebuilt strict verifier still rejects CPU Q8 at code step-2 logits
(**1.175e-5**) and the long-prompt layer 9 (**2.523e-5**), and CUDA BF16
at code step-2 logits (**1.098e-5**); normalized maxima are below **1e-4**.
New release-mode real-checkpoint tests nevertheless matched **24/24 greedy
IDs** for Q8 against the independently quantized HF reference and **24/24**
for CUDA BF16 against HF. Q8 prompt replay/split prefill, reset, isolation
and invalid-token rollback passed on all three prompts. CUDA whole/split,
reset, isolation, later-chunk rollback and code-prompt state replay passed.
The prior 23/24 Q8-versus-BF16 HF greedy agreement is unchanged; executable
code correctness remains unverified, so the full Q8 quality gate is open.

### Matched final-source performance

Serial release runs on the Ryzen 7 260 / RTX 5050 laptop used identical
synthetic prompt-token workloads, `--ctx 768 --tokens 8 --runs 2 --warmup 0`,
warm file cache and one CPU thread except the two marked 8-thread rows.
Raw JSON is in ignored `bench-results/granite/closure-*.json`. TTFT is the
median with the two-run range; GPU VRAM is **device-wide**, not process-only.
No process energy reading was available in these runs.

| Tokens, backend | TTFT s (range) | Prefill tok/s | Decode tok/s | Load s | Peak RSS GB | VRAM GB |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 128 CPU BF16, 1t | 1.072 (1.067–1.076) | 119.5 | 27.6 | 1.93 | 2.73 | — |
| 128 CPU Q8, 1t | 1.083 (1.077–1.089) | 118.2 | 49.7 | 1.40 | 1.43 | — |
| 128 CUDA BF16 | 0.261 (0.249–0.273) | 492.0 | 189.7 | 1.59 | 0.31 | 3.68 |
| 512 CPU BF16, 1t | 3.953 (3.932–3.974) | 129.5 | 26.4 | 1.70 | 2.78 | — |
| 512 CPU Q8, 1t | 4.200 (4.199–4.202) | 121.9 | 44.9 | 1.22 | 1.49 | — |
| 512 CUDA BF16 | 1.362 (1.304–1.419) | 376.7 | 148.7 | 1.57 | 0.31 | 3.79 |
| 512 CPU BF16, 8t | 1.285 (1.278–1.292) | 398.5 | 26.3 | 1.92 | 2.79 | — |
| 512 CPU Q8, 8t | 1.266 (1.265–1.268) | 404.4 | 44.3 | 1.19 | 1.49 | — |

Q8 retains **94.1%** of matched one-thread BF16 512 prefill, above its
original **90% of 131.3 tok/s** floor, and **101.5%** of matched 8-thread
BF16. Decode is **1.70× / 1.68×** BF16 at 1/8 threads; 512 RSS is **46.4%**
lower. CUDA 512 prefill/decode are **2.91× / 5.63×** one-thread CPU BF16,
and 128 TTFT is **4.11×** shorter. Both efficiency gates pass; numerical
gates independently fail.

| Path | Numerical decision | State/greedy | Efficiency decision |
|---|---|---|---|
| CPU Q8 | **NO-GO:** long layer 9 and code logits exceed 1e-5. | **PASS:** 24/24 quantized-HF greedy IDs, replay/reset/isolation/rollback. | **PASS:** speed, artifact and RSS gates; executable-code quality open. |
| CUDA BF16 | **NO-GO for default:** code-step-2 logits exceed 1e-5. | **PASS:** 24/24 HF greedy IDs and real state tests. | **PASS:** matched speed/fit gates; explicit opt-in stays experimental. |

The forced scalar Q8 path also matched **24/24** quantized-HF greedy IDs and
passed its real-model replay/reset/isolation/rollback test. Its strict
verifier still fails at code step-2 expert layer 1 (**1.327e-5 RMS**) and
long-prompt expert layer 6 (**1.256e-5 RMS**), confirming that disabling
AVX2 does not close the numerical gate. `MARIN_MATMUL_SCALAR=1` unit checks
passed **29 model and 37 tensor tests**. Final-source `MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1
cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` passed **630 tests, 0
failures** (five opt-in real-checkpoint tests ignored by default); all five
official Granite real-checkpoint tests passed separately in release mode.
`cargo fmt --all -- --check`, workspace/all-target `cargo check`, all-feature
Clippy with `-D warnings`, line-limit, unsafe and `git diff --check` passed.
The three stashes and all pre-existing working-tree changes were preserved;
no commit or push was made.

The smallest useful *next* larger-MoE residency experiment is an offline
route-trace and transfer pilot for Granite 4.0 H-Tiny on a **16 GiB RAM /
8 GiB VRAM** machine: use a trusted reference to record per-layer expert IDs
for prose/code, model exact Q8 expert bytes against 8/16/32-expert GPU
residency, and measure actual host-to-GPU miss bytes/latency. §43 estimates
about **7.2 GB** all-resident Q8 before state and safety, beyond the free
RTX 5050 VRAM, while the 16 GiB host can hold it. A cache is justified only
if observed hits and transfer cost beat all-resident CPU under an admitted
GPU budget. This is a proposed measurement, not a Marin implementation or
a claim that H-Tiny inference already works here.

## 46. Granite 4.0 H-Tiny expert-residency feasibility (2026-10-15)

### Acceptance gates fixed before measurement

This is an offline feasibility study, not a Marin inference or cache feature.
The official BF16 checkpoint and Transformers implementation define routing;
Granite 3.0 routes and numerical results are not substitutes. The decisions
below require evidence on the RTX 5050 laptop under its actual available
memory, with 512 MiB reserved VRAM and at least 2 GiB host headroom:

1. **Trace validity:** record actual HF per-layer top-6 expert IDs for prose
   and code, short and long prompts, and generated tokens. Verify 40 layers,
   six IDs per token per layer, valid IDs 0–63, and deterministic replay.
   Incomplete or synthetic routes cannot pass this gate.
2. **Fit:** inspect actual checkpoint tensor shapes and bytes. Admit the dense
   and shared weights, recurrent/KV state, workspace, cache metadata, staging,
   and safety reserve before any 8/16/32-expert-per-layer scenario. Host RSS
   must remain below usable RAM; device allocation below free VRAM minus
   reserve. Do not assume a future Q8 format preserves BF16 quality.
3. **Transfers:** time real page-locked host-to-device transfers of exact
   expert-sized buffers on this GPU, including staging and synchronization.
   Report cold/warm and p50/p95; theoretical PCIe bandwidth is not evidence.
4. **Residency:** replay exact traces with per-layer LRU at 8, 16, and 32
   slots. Count cold/warm hits, bytes moved, and serial transfer time; bound
   any proposed overlap by measured useful layer compute, never assume full
   hiding of transfers. The chosen policy must fit both memory budgets.
5. **Usefulness:** a GO for implementing residency requires a measured
   all-resident H-Tiny CPU baseline on matched prompts and a transfer-aware
   partial-GPU estimate of at least 20% better decode throughput, no more
   than 10% worse short-prompt TTFT, and at least 10 decode tok/s, while
   meeting fit. Without the real route trace, transfer data, or CPU baseline,
   classify the implementation decision BLOCKED rather than infer a win.

The pilot is time-boxed: inspect the official checkpoint and use focused
traces/transfers; stop at a blocker instead of building inference machinery.

### Official architecture and exact checkpoint inventory

Inspected [IBM's config](https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-4.0-h-tiny/blob/main/config.json),
[checkpoint index](https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-4.0-h-tiny/blob/main/model.safetensors.index.json),
and all three safetensors headers with HTTP byte ranges, pinned to HF revision
`791e0d3d28c86e106c9b6e0b4cecdee0375b6124`. These are **actual BF16
tensor shapes and byte counts**, not a loaded-model RSS measurement.

| Component | Official layout | BF16 bytes |
|---|---|---:|
| Routed experts | 40 layers × 64 experts; per expert input `[1024,1536]` and output `[1536,512]` | 12,079,595,520 (11.25 GiB) |
| Everything else | embedding/tied head, 36 Mamba2 + 4 attention mixers, 40 shared MLPs, routers, norms | 1,798,478,976 (1.675 GiB) |
| Complete checkpoint | 3 shards, 6,939,037,248 parameters | 13,878,074,496 (12.925 GiB) |

The 40 layers use Mamba2 except full attention at indices **5, 15, 25,
35**. Each has top-6 routing over 64 experts, a shared MLP, and exact
SwiGLU expert aggregation. The config has hidden width 1536, expert width
512, no positional embedding, tied output weights, and 128K maximum context.
The official [Transformers implementation](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/main/src/transformers/models/granitemoehybrid/modeling_granitemoehybrid.py)
uses a Mamba2 selective scan and causal convolution, distinct from Qwen3.5
DeltaNet, and exposes router logits before `topk`. The 36 recurrent states
and four ordinary attention KV caches are separate. A BF16 per-token
recurrent-state estimate is 27.9 MiB plus 1/4/16 MiB KV at 128/512/2048
tokens; FP32 state could double these figures. These are shape-derived
estimates, not runtime measurements. A top-6 token touches at most 240
expert weights: **1,132 MB BF16** or **584 MB estimated Q8**, before reuse.

Marin's Granite 3.0 code already has router selection, shared tensor/CPU
Q8 operations, all-resident CUDA expert execution, and V9 admission
accounting. Its `granitemoe` config, full-attention layer execution, and
weight names cannot parse or execute `granitemoehybrid`. H-Tiny needs a
validated Mamba2 mixer/convolution/state path, NoPE attention semantics,
shared MLP, and grouped expert tensor loading before a CPU or GPU baseline
can exist. No Granite 3.0 numerical NO-GO status changes here.

### Prospective RAM/VRAM fit, not an admitted runtime

For a *hypothetical* Marin group-128 Q8 artifact, the exact expert tensor
elements imply 2,359,296 signed codes + 73,728 FP32 scale bytes =
**2,433,024 bytes per expert**, 6,228,541,440 bytes for all experts.
Keeping all other checkpoint tensors BF16 gives **7.475 GiB host weights**;
this deliberately avoids assuming that Mamba/router/shared weights can be
quantized without quality loss. It is a format-size estimate, not an
exported artifact or an accepted quantization quality result.

At measurement time the RTX 5050 had **8,151 MiB total, 6,287 MiB free**;
the host had **14 GiB physical, about 11 GiB available, 4 GiB swap**.
The table budgets BF16 dense/shared GPU weights (1.675 GiB), the listed
expert cache, **512 MiB provisional GPU workspace/state** and **512 MiB GPU
safety**. Host needs 7.475 GiB weights + at least 2 GiB safety + runtime,
staging and OS use; this fits the observed 11 GiB available only if those
other costs stay below about 1.5 GiB. These allowances are *planning
assumptions*, not a validated admission bound.

| Slots/layer | BF16 expert cache | BF16 GPU plan | Q8 expert cache | Q8 GPU plan | Preliminary fit at 6,287 MiB free |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 8 | 1.406 GiB | 4.081 GiB | 0.725 GiB | 3.400 GiB | both possible |
| 16 | 2.812 GiB | 5.487 GiB | 1.450 GiB | 4.125 GiB | both possible; BF16 tight |
| 32 | 5.625 GiB | 8.300 GiB | 2.900 GiB | 5.575 GiB | BF16 rejects; Q8 possible with only 0.56 GiB beyond reserves |

All-resident BF16 exceeds host and VRAM budgets. Even the roughly 6.49 GiB
*all-weight* Q8 estimate from §43 exceeds the currently free VRAM after the
512 MiB reserve, before state/workspace. None of these capacities establishes
that H-Tiny Q8 preserves output quality.

The comparison is therefore asymmetric: all-resident BF16 CPU is rejected
by current host headroom; all-resident **Q8 CPU** and **partial-Q8 GPU** are
plausible *fit scenarios* but have no H-Tiny implementation, quality result,
or matched timing. BF16 GPU caching also requires keeping 11.25 GiB of
experts in host RAM and fails the 2 GiB host safety budget here, regardless
of whether 8 or 16 GPU slots fit. A CPU/GPU hybrid that computes misses on
the CPU instead of transferring them could save PCIe time but would need
measured CPU expert cost and synchronized accumulation; neither is available.
Consequently no CPU-vs-GPU crossover or smallest winning slot count can be
selected from capacity alone.

### Measured transfer pilot and unmeasured routing

An out-of-tree CUDA runtime microbenchmark allocated one pinned host staging
buffer and one device destination, rotated through 64 distinct RAM-resident
pageable source buffers, copied into the pinned buffer, issued an asynchronous
host-to-device copy, then synchronized. After 32 warmups, **224 samples**
per size on RTX 5050 PCIe **Gen4 ×8**, SM about 1.95–2.0 GHz, 54 °C:

| Payload | Pageable→pinned p50/p95 | Pinned→GPU + sync p50/p95 | Full miss p50/p95 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Group-128 Q8 expert, 2,433,024 B | 0.114/0.191 ms | 0.181/0.213 ms | **0.295/0.386 ms** |
| BF16 expert, 4,718,592 B | 0.276/0.479 ms | 0.343/0.391 ms | **0.626/0.899 ms** |

The measured uploads correspond to about 13.4–13.8 GB/s; a *miss* must
include staging and synchronization unless future measurements prove a
different overlap. This experiment excludes checkpoint/disk reads, cache
management, expert computation, and CPU↔GPU router synchronization; it is
neither end-to-end latency nor a model throughput benchmark.

For sensitivity only, 240 selected expert occurrences per decode token
would cost **70.7 ms Q8 / 150.3 ms BF16** at zero hits, or **35.4 / 75.1
ms** at 50% hits, if every miss serialized at the median full-miss cost.
This is arithmetic on measured transfer times, **not** observed H-Tiny
routing or a cache simulation. A 512-token prefill may batch each distinct
expert once per layer, but actual unique sets and evictions remain unknown.
Colibrì's [per-layer LRU and pinned hot-store](https://github.com/uv-genai/colibri)
motivate measuring locality; its GLM routing and published throughput cannot
be transferred to H-Tiny. Within a layer, uploads might overlap shared-MLP
or other already-ready expert work after routing; across layers, the next
router depends on the current layer output. No overlap is credited here.

The official BF16 checkpoint was **not** already local. Both ordinary and
HF chunked downloads were stopped after a few minutes when throughput was
about 1–1.6 MB/s, implying hours for 13.9 GB. Only config/index and tensor
headers were acquired; no model weight values, HF routes, or generated text
were loaded. A temporary HF/PyTorch environment installation was also stopped
to avoid extending this focused pilot. Consequently there are **no real
prose/code or generation routes, LRU hit rates, per-layer hot-set sizes, CPU
H-Tiny baseline, TTFT, decode TPS, process RSS/VRAM, or cold/warm-cache
measurements**. No synthetic routes are presented as replacements.

| Gate | Decision | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Official structure/footprint | PASS | Revision-pinned config, index, and 3 shard headers; exact counts above. |
| Real HF routing/replay | BLOCKED | Complete checkpoint unavailable within time-box. |
| Host/GPU runtime admission | BLOCKED | Header-derived budgets only; workspace and Q8 quality unmeasured. |
| Actual pinned expert transfers | PASS for microbenchmark | Exact BF16 and group-128 Q8 payloads; staging and sync included. |
| 8/16/32 trace-driven LRU | BLOCKED | No real route IDs; capacity table is not a hit-rate simulation. |
| CPU crossover and usefulness | BLOCKED | No all-resident H-Tiny CPU baseline or H-Tiny GPU compute timing. |

**Implementation decision: NO-GO now; feasibility remains BLOCKED.** The
smallest next experiment is to stage the official checkpoint on a faster
connection or suitable machine, record HF top-6 IDs and a matched CPU
baseline, then replay the IDs through an offline per-layer LRU at 8/16/32
slots using these measured miss costs. Only if a policy clears the fixed
speed, TTFT and memory gates should Marin gain a prototype expert tier.
There is no production cache, SSD streaming, prefetching, INT4, scheduler,
or new model implementation in this study.

